    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As immobile organisms, it is essential for plants to be able to adapt readily to changing environments and particularly to threats posed by antagonistic herbivores. Contributing to this necessary flexibility may be the ability of plants to prime their induced defenses, (those that are raised in response to herbivores rather than innately found in the plant) through previous inductions across multiple generations or through volatile organic compound communication by neighbors. While studies in these areas have been conducted before, little work has been done specifically on the wild radish plant, Raphanus raphanistrum, or on the possible ecological consequences of this phenomenon. Palatability was used as a way to test the concrete benefits of the transgenerational phenotypic changes on a plant’s survival and fitness, and evidence for communication was found by examining whether defenses were induced in plants not directly exposed to herbivores, but in the vicinity of individuals who were being depredated. We constructed a palatability index through a series of lab trials to determine herbivore preference and consequently each individual plant’s palatability and a manipulative greenhouse experiment to examine communication capabilities. Heritable epigenetic changes and their effects on palatability as well as plant-plant communication demonstrate how truly adaptable plants can be and may have serious implications for the way we understand plant ecology and evolution in the future.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
